00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:14.720
- Right.
- It's like, what, what?

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:15.820
Some sort of tax

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:17.160
- Like Trump.
- Ploy?

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:17.993
- Yeah.

00:31:17.993 --> 00:31:18.826
- What happened?

00:31:18.826 --> 00:31:19.860
- Yeah.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.300
- So that was literally my number one.

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:24.368
If you were to put your number one, is it

00:31:24.368 --> 00:31:25.486
restaurants?
- No, it's not number one.

00:31:25.486 --> 00:31:30.486
It's not number one because I
really like my new television.

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:32.710
You know, I finally did the
- Yeah.

00:31:32.710 --> 00:31:35.450
- I finally did the 4K OLED

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:37.183
television.
- Yeah.

00:31:37.183 --> 00:31:39.150
So what is your number one?

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:39.983
- Eating.

00:31:39.983 --> 00:31:41.653
I mean restaurants.
- Restaurants.

00:31:42.577 --> 00:31:44.530
- And there's just something
about eating at a restaurant

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:46.450
versus getting takeout from a restaurant.

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:47.283
- Oh yeah.

00:31:47.283 --> 00:31:48.930
I mean, I've really forgotten.

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:51.227
I think I'm gonna go to
a restaurant and be like,

00:31:51.227 --> 00:31:55.210
"Oh yeah, this should
have been my number one."

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:57.700
The food tastes so much better.

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:01.330
We supported our favorite
restaurants locally

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:05.530
and they'd send you their
signature dishes deconstructed,

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:06.910
- Yeah.
- so that everything

00:32:06.910 --> 00:32:08.700
doesn't over melt together.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:12.243
- You lose the presentation of the food.

00:32:13.132 --> 00:32:15.360
And many times you lose
the freshness of the food

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:17.980
'cause it's coming to you in a bag.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:20.420
You also lose, a big part of it for me,

00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:21.470
is just the ambience.

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:24.560
I mean, for me eating is about

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:27.570
all the senses being stimulated, right?

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:30.000
And I love eating at restaurants.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.540
I'd love the entire experience.

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:33.830
And yes, I understand
that there are restaurants

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.430
that are open for outdoor dining

00:32:36.691 --> 00:32:38.650
and that they're socially
distanced dining--

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:39.930
- We haven't doing that.

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:44.640
- Right, so because we've been following

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:47.890
heightened protocols
associated with production

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:50.660
so that our limited crew,

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:53.078
we can all protect each other and continue

00:32:53.078 --> 00:32:55.020
you know, making our shows.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:57.300
- Our standards are higher
than just the general

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:59.100
California standards
which are already higher

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:00.960
than the general sort
of national standard.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:03.240
- So we're admittedly behind.
- Yeah.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:04.620
But even in my own brain

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:06.980
I've locked off the restaurant zone

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:10.010
because it hurt too
much after a few months

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:12.640
at the beginning of the
pandemic to dwell on it.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:14.900
So now I'm finding it difficult

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:17.460
to reopen those compartments.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:19.740
But I think when I go to a restaurant

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:23.770
the other part I miss
is that, yes, you still

00:33:23.770 --> 00:33:26.933
got the whole phone
situation in your pocket,

00:33:29.435 --> 00:33:31.120
but how it sets the stage for connection

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:33.703
you stare across the table at somebody,

00:33:33.703 --> 00:33:35.542
me and Christie on a date,

00:33:35.542 --> 00:33:39.230
a date is much different
out than trying to

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:41.050
recreate it in your own home.

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:43.660
Matter of fact, we just
got to a point where

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:45.360
we thought we were going
to do the date thing

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:46.640
and then it just didn't happen.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:47.550
- Yeah, we did it.

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:48.843
We held on for a little bit.

00:33:48.843 --> 00:33:49.676
- It just didn't happen.

00:33:49.676 --> 00:33:51.440
- We did a thing for a
while where we would,

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:56.963
I would turn the lights
down, put on some music

00:33:56.963 --> 00:34:01.680
and we would eat at our
little sort of breakfast nook.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:04.050
And then we would make the
kids eat in a different room

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:05.413
or like eat in the dining room and--

00:34:05.413 --> 00:34:06.246
- Right?

00:34:06.246 --> 00:34:07.360
- I don't know, it worked for a while,

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:11.070
but it's just so hard to
maintain the separation

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:12.120
and the discipline.

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:12.953
- Yeah.

00:34:12.953 --> 00:34:15.200
And then you're still doing
the dishes and the preparation.

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:16.130
- Yeah, right.

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:17.680
- Even if you order out.
- Yeah.

00:34:17.680 --> 00:34:20.290
You know, it's like just being waited on

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:22.870
in a restaurant is so awesome.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.090
- And yeah, I mean, I
love the experience--

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:28.470
- And it's like, I mean, I'm
starting to get excited now.

00:34:28.470 --> 00:34:30.470
Cause it's like, I mean I think

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:32.140
I'm going to be at a restaurant

00:34:32.140 --> 00:34:33.710
and I'm gonna be like,
"Wow, I feel like a King!"

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:36.310
Like, there'll be just
this renewed appreciation

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:37.143
for the whole thing.

00:34:37.143 --> 00:34:39.510
Look, someone's coming up,
and just anything I want,

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.810
well and I can just tell this stranger

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.730
and they'll go away and
they'll come back with it!

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:47.430
- And that's why, I mean,
one of the many reasons

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:51.100
that the cost of living
in Los Angeles is so high,

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:56.100
you know compared to North
Carolina for instance,

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:03.170
is the event, like we're
so close to so many things

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:06.630
including so many great places to eat.

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:10.810
Like there's just so
many, you can never eat

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:13.780
at the same place twice
and keep having these

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:19.040
completely dynamic excellent
dining experiences.

00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:19.925
Now I'm not saying you can't get it--

00:35:19.925 --> 00:35:22.150
Well, maybe one of your
favorite things in LA.

00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:22.983
- Yeah.
- As well.

00:35:22.983 --> 00:35:24.440
- It's, I mean, it's one
of the things I'm like

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:27.550
when I think about how much
money I paid to live here,

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:30.580
I'm like, you kind of have
to start reminding yourself

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:33.120
of the advantages, because
especially one of the reasons

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:36.530
there's been this mass Exodus
of people from Los Angeles

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.370
during the pandemic is
because they're like,

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:41.010
if all I'm doing is hanging out at home,

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:45.410
why am I going to be in this
tiny little expensive place

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:48.160
when I can go to a less
expensive, larger place

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:51.200
and have the same experience, right?

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.240
I totally get it.

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:56.470
I dunno, we're not moving because
we've built our lives here

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:58.230
and we built our business
here but it's like,

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:00.380
I'm ready to experience the city

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:01.660
that I'm paying so much for.

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:06.140
- Well, I mean, I came so
close to buying a van that,

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:08.040
you know, I wouldn't have lived out of it,

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:12.100
but I would travel in semi-live
out of it on little trips.

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:13.126
And,

00:36:13.126 --> 00:36:14.110
(Link guffaws)

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:15.610
that's taken a back burner now.

00:36:15.610 --> 00:36:17.450
It's like, I can rent somebody else's van

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:18.930
whenever I want to do that.
- Yeah.

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:20.680
- But yeah.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.700
So that's the update on that aspiration.

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:27.123
It was, I had the tunnel
vision of the pandemic goggles,

00:36:28.176 --> 00:36:31.850
you know, and I'm, I'm
gonna rent a van again.

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:32.810
Imma, trust me.

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:36.030
I'm gonna do it, but I'm not, I don't

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:37.990
I'm pretty sure I'm not going to buy one.

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:41.130
- The other aspect of this is parties.

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:43.002
Now again--
- Before we get to par--

00:36:43.002 --> 00:36:45.140
- Let's go with the friends.

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:46.670
Cause I think that's

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:47.503
the incremental thing--
- That's what I'm saying.

00:36:47.503 --> 00:36:48.760
That's what I'm saying,
there's two aspects of this.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:51.056
And so I'm saying that the first part

00:36:51.056 --> 00:36:53.750
which I've experienced a little bit of

00:36:53.750 --> 00:36:57.930
is getting together at
people's homes, right.

00:36:57.930 --> 00:36:59.820
Now, very recently.
- Yep.

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:05.980
- So we got together
with another well, okay.

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:10.060
So there's a couple that
was fully vaccinated,

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:14.350
Jesse and I are, you know,
Jesse's fully vaccinated now,

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:15.617
I'm half vaccinated.

00:37:15.617 --> 00:37:17.430
And you know, once you're a few weeks out

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:19.855
from the initial dose of
these Moderna or Pfizer,

00:37:19.855 --> 00:37:23.160
you're very well-protected, especially if

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.780
you're around other people
who are vaccinated, right?

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:29.250
And so we made, so
there's we see each other

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:33.430
and we see this one other family,
but there was a friend who

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:38.430
we hadn't seen in over a year
who was at this family's house

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:44.375
and texted us and said,
"Hey, I'm over here."

00:37:44.375 --> 00:37:46.650
"I'm fully vaccinated."
- I'm fully vaccinated.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:49.080
- They're totally cool if guys come over

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:52.040
and it was a Friday night and--

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.083
- It was probably, it was like

00:37:54.083 --> 00:37:56.830
it was like 7:30 or 8 pm.
- It was like eight.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:58.540
I already, here's the thing.

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:00.950
I already, I had my plan.

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:03.970
And my plan was we were
going to watch a movie

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:06.560
and I don't remember what it
was, but I had it picked out.

00:38:06.560 --> 00:38:08.327
And then we got the texts and I was like,

00:38:08.327 --> 00:38:09.947
"Ah, I kind of had this plan,

00:38:09.947 --> 00:38:12.071
"but I haven't seen
this person in a year."

00:38:12.071 --> 00:38:14.670
Yes, let's go.

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:16.600
And we go over there.

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:21.440
And I mean, again, there's like hugs,

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:25.200
first thing was we're hugging.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:28.020
This is a little bit unusual, right?

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:29.990
Just, I haven't hugged a lot of people--

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:32.160
- Yeah.
- In a long time!

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:33.580
- Now you're not a hugger.

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:38.340
So when that happened,
what was your experience?

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:40.270
Because there are people who are huggers

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:42.700
and they're like that might
be the top of their list

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:44.990
to have like physical connection with--

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:46.847
- I'm not a--
- Friends.

00:38:46.847 --> 00:38:50.068
I'm not a hug instigator,
but I'm not an anti--

00:38:50.068 --> 00:38:50.901
I'm not--
- No you're not...

00:38:50.901 --> 00:38:51.734
- I'm not a anti I'm the anti-hug.

00:38:51.734 --> 00:38:52.810
You, but you're not--
- I'm a hugger.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:54.920
- So I'm a good hug receiver.
- Right.

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:57.700
- And like, I'm a good hug reciprocator.

00:38:57.700 --> 00:38:59.560
- You're kind of neutral.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.540
You can, I think you
can take it or leave it.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:03.272
I think I could too.

00:39:03.272 --> 00:39:04.490
No judgment here.
- Uh...

00:39:04.490 --> 00:39:05.660
How was it?

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:07.420
- So given that you could take or leave

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:11.300
a hug in normal life, was it therapeutic?

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:12.810
Was there a release?
- I don't want--

00:39:12.810 --> 00:39:14.090
- Or was it the same?

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:16.730
- I'm not gonna, I don't
want to be dramatic.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:17.563
- Don't be dramatic.

00:39:17.563 --> 00:39:18.510
I'm just saying--
- Be honest.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:21.260
- Yeah, I'm saying that
I'm going to be honest

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:24.170
and say that it was really good,

00:39:24.170 --> 00:39:28.637
but it wasn't like,
"Man, that feels good!"

00:39:28.637 --> 00:39:29.930
"That feels different!"

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:32.400
That's like a giant step forward.

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:34.690
It did not feel that way for me,

00:39:34.690 --> 00:39:38.300
it just felt, in fact, as we then sat down

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:43.300
and began to have a conversation
around the kitchen table

00:39:43.454 --> 00:39:48.170
it began very quickly to
feel like old times again.

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:51.090
And I actually consciously
sort of stepped out

00:39:51.090 --> 00:39:52.990
of the conversation and
went into my own head

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:56.910
and thought to myself,
like, "This is interesting,"

00:39:56.910 --> 00:40:01.910
because I feel like
I'm totally here again.

00:40:03.570 --> 00:40:08.570
Like I feel like the past
year is just a memory.

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:11.530
Now I know again, it's
going to be a slow process

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:13.650
of getting back to normal.

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:16.700
And I know this is not,
I say this, you know

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:18.620
being sensitive to the fact
that this is not the case

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:21.120
for a lot of people, but
it just kind of hit me.

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:25.457
I was like, "Hmm, I'm immediately back

00:40:25.457 --> 00:40:29.217
"in the same mind space and social space

00:40:29.217 --> 00:40:32.117
"and social experience that I
was before all this happened,

00:40:32.117 --> 00:40:34.430
"like having this conversation."

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:37.840
But I could tell that not everyone

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:40.720
at that table felt the same way.

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:45.050
- And you should also
say who from my family

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:46.460
was or wasn't at that table.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:50.360
So we began to talk about
you, because you and Christie

00:40:50.360 --> 00:40:53.351
had also received the text
from this person to come over.

00:40:53.351 --> 00:40:54.184
- Yep.

00:40:54.184 --> 00:40:58.540
- And there was a conversation
and the conversation was,

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:01.237
they were like, "Are you thinking

00:41:01.237 --> 00:41:02.070
"Link and Christie are coming?"

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:02.903
I was like, "Well"--

00:41:02.903 --> 00:41:03.736
- This is between you and Jesse?

00:41:03.736 --> 00:41:04.960
Are you already at the--

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:06.450
- Already at the table.

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:09.027
I was like, "Well, you
know, Link and Christie,

00:41:09.027 --> 00:41:11.357
"they, the Neal-plan
may already be in effect

00:41:11.357 --> 00:41:14.430
"and this may be difficult for
them to dislodge from this."

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:15.920
And then we had a conversation--

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:17.930
- And you pretty much
knew what that plan was.

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:20.440
- Well, and I'll just say
it's funny because, okay,

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:22.943
so it was, it was the McHargue's house.

00:41:22.943 --> 00:41:27.943
And because Mike said,
"Well, here's the spectrum."

00:41:28.907 --> 00:41:31.940
"On this end of the
spectrum in terms of..."

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:33.550
I can't remember the term he used,

00:41:33.550 --> 00:41:37.370
but it was basically
like social flexibility

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:38.203
or something like that.

00:41:38.203 --> 00:41:41.320
He was like, "You have the
Neals and the McHargues."

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:43.280
- Yup, they gather on one end.

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:44.113
- And at the opposite--

00:41:44.113 --> 00:41:46.040
- Of the spectrum...
- Very socially inflexible.

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:47.805
- You have the McLaughlin's.

00:41:47.805 --> 00:41:51.387
- And I was like, "Well,
let me butt in and say,

00:41:51.387 --> 00:41:52.677
"That is true."

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:56.720
The McLaughlin's and the person
who was the guests there.

00:41:58.220 --> 00:42:00.167
I was like, "That's true of my wife

00:42:00.167 --> 00:42:02.390
"but I'm kind of in the middle."

00:42:02.390 --> 00:42:03.880
I kind of straddled the fence on this one

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:06.240
because I will admit that
it was not easy for me

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:07.860
to just drop what my wanted to do

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:09.377
and watch a movie and come over.

00:42:09.377 --> 00:42:11.970
I tend to kind of get into a plan.

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:14.150
And as soon as we finished
that conversation,

00:42:14.150 --> 00:42:19.150
knock, knock, knock, your
wife and daughter show up,

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:22.410
but noticeably you're not there.

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:23.873
- Yeah.

00:42:23.873 --> 00:42:25.230
(Rhett scoffs)

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:27.453
- So just to rewind a little bit.

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:31.270
Yeah, we're, we're sitting down like we,

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:33.520
it's eight o'clock-ish.

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:36.530
So all the Neal's without prompting now,

00:42:36.530 --> 00:42:40.380
we all gather on, in
the TV room on the couch

00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:43.567
and we put on "Survivor,"
every single night!

00:42:43.567 --> 00:42:45.610
- Ooh well, on Friday
night you're having this?

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:48.435
- Even Friday night!
- Wow.

00:42:48.435 --> 00:42:52.177
- I gave up a long time
ago on trying to like say,

00:42:52.177 --> 00:42:54.220
"Yeah but on the weekends,
we're not doing "Survivor."

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:55.730
That's a weeknight thing on the weekends

00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:57.320
we're going to do our own things

00:42:57.320 --> 00:42:59.240
or we're going to watch movies

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:01.660
or it's gonna it's gonna
feel like a normal weekend,

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:03.160
not just like another night

00:43:03.160 --> 00:43:05.428
like every single night
we watch "Survivor".

00:43:05.428 --> 00:43:06.261
(Link guffaws)
- Friday nights?

00:43:06.261 --> 00:43:07.094
- I mean, even,--
- With your kids?

00:43:07.094 --> 00:43:09.340
- Even me, I was like,
"This is too much, guys."

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:11.590
And I lost, I lost the argument.

00:43:11.590 --> 00:43:12.920
And then I was okay with that.

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:13.753
- Okay.

00:43:13.753 --> 00:43:16.490
- And then months later
I'm really okay with it.

00:43:16.490 --> 00:43:19.600
And then this, this one night,
it's like any other night

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:23.180
for the past year, we
look forward to this.

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:24.240
We relish in it.

00:43:24.240 --> 00:43:28.260
If, you know, if anything breaks that up

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:31.610
just within the home, somebody gets mad.

00:43:31.610 --> 00:43:32.640
You know?

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:35.920
It's like, if somebody is
not as into "WandaVision"

00:43:35.920 --> 00:43:39.377
as the rest of the family,
introducing, like putting

00:43:39.377 --> 00:43:43.710
"WandaVision" above "Survivor"
or, has been a problem.

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:45.080
- Yeah, how do you watch other things?

00:43:45.080 --> 00:43:45.913
Or how do you watch movies?

00:43:45.913 --> 00:43:48.319
- We double up, we don't watch movies.

00:43:48.319 --> 00:43:49.152
(Rhett scoffs)
We watch, we watch--

00:43:49.152 --> 00:43:51.000
- We don't watch movies,
I'll just be honest with you.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:54.210
- If it's whatever's on
Disney + that we're all,

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.910
the new series that we're all gonna watch,

00:43:55.910 --> 00:43:57.370
we watch that on Friday and then

00:43:57.370 --> 00:43:58.870
we watch "Survivor" afterwards.

00:43:58.870 --> 00:44:01.570
(Rhett laughing)
- It's ridiculous.

00:44:01.570 --> 00:44:02.930
I know it is.

00:44:02.930 --> 00:44:03.996
We're so extreme in this way--

00:44:03.996 --> 00:44:05.340
- What're you gonna do, at this pace,

00:44:05.340 --> 00:44:06.750
you're definitely gonna run out.

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:11.510
- We are halfway through
the Season 39 finale.

00:44:11.510 --> 00:44:13.470
- How many seasons are there?
- 40.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:14.660
- So you're getting
close to running out...

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:17.400
- And last night we, we paused it

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:19.780
at a strategic halfway point of the finale

00:44:19.780 --> 00:44:21.594
because it was getting to
be, it's a school night.

00:44:21.594 --> 00:44:23.080
It's getting to be bedtime.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:27.580
And I'm like, "Guys, I
don't think we should go."

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:29.100
Like tomorrow night we're going to watch

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:30.340
the rest of the finale.

00:44:30.340 --> 00:44:32.210
And then the next night,
I know you're going

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:33.920
to want to just start with season 40.

00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:35.640
That's the last one.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:39.128
And we've heard that it's
like the best season.

00:44:39.128 --> 00:44:41.680
I don't want to go to it.

00:44:41.680 --> 00:44:43.300
I want, I wanna wait.

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:44.133
(Link yells)

00:44:44.133 --> 00:44:46.130
The complaining starts like,
"No dad, you're being crazy."

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:48.030
And it's like, there are some episodes,

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:49.850
seasons that we skipped.

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:52.920
I think we should delay the
gratification of Season 40

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:54.190
go back and watch some of the...

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:55.023
We're so into it.

00:44:55.023 --> 00:44:56.240
Now let's watch some of the seasons

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:57.570
that people said sucked.

00:44:57.570 --> 00:44:59.190
- Yeah, you've become dependent.

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:01.850
- We are dependent on
"Survivor" every single night--

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:02.683
- To survive.

00:45:02.683 --> 00:45:04.980
- So I'm like, listen, we're going to blow

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:08.390
through season 40 in two weeks.

00:45:08.390 --> 00:45:10.050
That's that's the pace we've been on.

00:45:10.050 --> 00:45:11.650
- Yeah.
- We kept increasing.

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:15.917
I mean, it is this like
dependent compulsion,

00:45:15.917 --> 00:45:20.550
leading to addiction kind of trajectory.

00:45:22.250 --> 00:45:23.517
- You're going to have to
figure out some sort of--

00:45:23.517 --> 00:45:25.950
- And I'm trying to point
that out to him last night.

00:45:25.950 --> 00:45:29.097
I'm like, "Listen, guys,
we need to delay season 40,

00:45:29.097 --> 00:45:31.660
"so that we can build it up
more and look forward to it."

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:33.740
Even if we skip three nights.

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:34.573
- Hmm.

00:45:34.573 --> 00:45:36.910
- I wanna keep that Season
40 dangling out there

00:45:36.910 --> 00:45:39.910
until it's closer to Season 41 coming out.

00:45:39.910 --> 00:45:41.500
And then I'm also talking about when Lily

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:45.120
goes off to college, I'm like, you know,

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.280
what are we going to do with "Survivor?"

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:48.310
She's like, "We'll just
watch it and talk about it."

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:49.420
I was like, no, no, no.

00:45:49.420 --> 00:45:50.580
We're gonna save "Survivor"

00:45:50.580 --> 00:45:52.524
for holidays when we're all back together,

00:45:52.524 --> 00:45:53.480
(Rhett scoffs)
and then we're gonna

00:45:53.480 --> 00:45:54.900
watch those seasons.

00:45:54.900 --> 00:45:57.550
This is how small my world is become.

00:45:57.550 --> 00:45:58.383
- Yeah.

00:45:58.383 --> 00:45:59.820
- Two weeks ago, we talked about

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.050
the wanting to look into communes.

00:46:02.050 --> 00:46:03.290
It was very tongue in cheek

00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:06.620
but it was rooted in
this feeling that I have

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:09.290
of I really liked the control

00:46:09.290 --> 00:46:12.323
and the perceived, is
different kinds of safety

00:46:12.323 --> 00:46:15.070
that has nothing to do
with COVID or pandemics

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:19.360
of just smallness of this
isn't this is my world now.

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:22.000
And this is, I know what to expect.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:27.000
And it is fostering some
things that aren't healthy

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:31.220
and that are, this is what I discovered.

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:33.123
And this was, this is a moment.

00:46:33.970 --> 00:46:35.140
It was a test.

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:39.300
We were sitting down and
then Christie looks up

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:43.437
and she's like, "Oh, we were invited over

00:46:43.437 --> 00:46:46.360
"to the McConnaughts to see so-and-so."

00:46:46.360 --> 00:46:49.890
And he's over there, now
he's fully vaccinated.

00:46:49.890 --> 00:46:52.550
And she locks eyes with me.

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:56.300
And I bet if you were to ask
her what she saw in my eyes

00:46:56.300 --> 00:46:58.410
I think it would be fear.
(Rhett laughing)

00:46:58.410 --> 00:47:01.800
And it had nothing to do with the friend

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:05.340
or how much I love and miss him.

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:09.040
It was, and it wasn't just
about a threat to the routine,

00:47:09.040 --> 00:47:12.470
but it was, things are changing.

00:47:12.470 --> 00:47:15.840
Now we had already
planned to get together.

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.180
This was a Friday night.

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:18.541
We had already planned to get together

00:47:18.541 --> 00:47:21.410
on Sunday night--
- Hm mm, at my house.

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:25.240
- And I was, I was getting
mentally prepared for that.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:26.073
- Hmm.

00:47:26.073 --> 00:47:26.990
- And then this was dropped in my lap

00:47:26.990 --> 00:47:30.030
and I'm like, well you know, I was just,

00:47:30.030 --> 00:47:32.340
I was getting ready for Sunday.

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:35.159
And now this thing right
now, right now, right now

00:47:35.159 --> 00:47:39.760
and there were some mitigating
circumstances in our house.

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:42.440
I had actually just gotten the vaccine

00:47:42.440 --> 00:47:47.340
the day before and experienced a headache.

00:47:47.340 --> 00:47:51.700
And it, then I thought, I, I
felt totally good that day,

00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:54.650
the second day, which
is the day in question.

00:47:54.650 --> 00:47:57.130
And I went for a bike
ride when I got back,

00:47:57.130 --> 00:47:58.650
the headache came back.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:00.470
I did too much too soon on day two,

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:01.850
shouldn't have done that.

00:48:01.850 --> 00:48:03.250
I actually didn't feel good.

00:48:04.330 --> 00:48:07.410
And you know, there were
other members of the family.

00:48:07.410 --> 00:48:10.550
You can figure it out by
matter of elimination that just

00:48:10.550 --> 00:48:12.803
that were reticent for their own reasons.

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:16.650
So that led to, I'm going
to stay at home with them

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:18.470
and we can, we can blame it on the kids

00:48:18.470 --> 00:48:20.517
but I have these things
going on inside of me.

00:48:20.517 --> 00:48:22.850
And I don't know what's happening

00:48:22.850 --> 00:48:27.653
in my brain and with my
emotions, but I just, I don't,

00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:31.290
I'm not willing or ready to do this.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:34.733
I'm not motivated enough to
overcome these feelings tonight.

00:48:35.812 --> 00:48:39.163
I'm working forward to
being ready by Sunday.

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:43.660
And then I ended up, we watched "Survivor"

00:48:43.660 --> 00:48:46.820
without Christie and Lily because
they gave us permission to

00:48:46.820 --> 00:48:48.770
and I felt dirty and horrible.
- Oh my goodness!

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:50.820
- And I said, it didn't feel right.

00:48:50.820 --> 00:48:52.380
And I don't want to ever do that again.

00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:55.306
And then I went to bed
and my head was killing me

00:48:55.306 --> 00:48:57.640
and I was so I was glad I wasn't there.

00:48:57.640 --> 00:49:00.960
I wouldn't have, I wouldn't
have been a positive contributor

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:03.750
to that reunion, even though I hate

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:05.630
that I didn't get to see our friend.

00:49:05.630 --> 00:49:07.803
And I, you know, I don't want him to think

00:49:07.803 --> 00:49:10.150
that it had anything to
do with him specifically

00:49:10.150 --> 00:49:12.920
but it really brought to light things

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:15.930
that I was just not
aware of at that moment.

00:49:15.930 --> 00:49:18.640
And then, you know, so we're getting,

00:49:18.640 --> 00:49:20.900
we can get back to the positives,
but we might as well go

00:49:20.900 --> 00:49:22.980
through this anxiety
because that's what I s--

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:26.320
I never thought, I never had

00:49:27.750 --> 00:49:30.350
what I would call "Social Anxiety."

00:49:30.350 --> 00:49:31.770
I don't think.

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:33.060
It's so hard to remember.

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:34.780
- So you think that
that's what it is for you?

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:35.750
- Well--
- Like, okay,

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:37.710
maybe that's one way to describe it, but--

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:42.400
- What if you were invited
to, let's not just say

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:44.610
it's a get-together with
people that you've know

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.691
and you've seen a bunch, and
then one additional person

00:49:47.691 --> 00:49:49.340
but an actual social thing.

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:50.801
Like a, like a party--

00:49:50.801 --> 00:49:53.800
- Like I do, even when
we have, when you did

00:49:53.800 --> 00:49:55.820
your monthly game night
and you had extent,

00:49:55.820 --> 00:49:58.323
like more friends show up
for that, including us--

00:49:58.323 --> 00:50:00.340
- Back in the day, pre-COVID?
- Pre-COVID.

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:02.860
There were sometimes I do notice

00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:05.560
that there's a little
heightened level of anxiety

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:08.570
that I experienced, associated
with, even with that.

00:50:08.570 --> 00:50:13.010
But some of it's just excitement
that can feel like anxiety.

00:50:13.010 --> 00:50:15.230
So I didn't really know what was going on

00:50:15.230 --> 00:50:18.083
but I would say that
there are plenty of times

00:50:18.083 --> 00:50:23.083
that I'm not totally
at ease, even with like

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:29.600
my second tier friends that
like I trust and enjoy and love.

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:32.100
So there was an inkling
of that, that I recall

00:50:32.100 --> 00:50:33.480
from pre-COVID.

00:50:33.480 --> 00:50:36.270
But now on Sunday night,
when we got together

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:38.140
I got to tell you the thing
that you saw when you looked

00:50:38.140 --> 00:50:40.810
around the table and you
could see in people's eyes

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:43.260
they were experiencing
something that you weren't

00:50:44.380 --> 00:50:48.060
you felt totally normal
and maybe exhilarated

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:49.270
to be back together.

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:51.980
Even on Sunday night,
when we all got together

00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:56.420
I actually felt when we left I
felt a little discouragement.

00:50:56.420 --> 00:50:59.420
You know, there's a lot
of self judgment that I,

00:50:59.420 --> 00:51:01.850
that tends to come into play with me,

00:51:01.850 --> 00:51:06.850
but there's I'm a lot
more in my head right now,

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:09.810
because of this whole re-entry thing--

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:11.620
- You were--
- Than I even realized.

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:14.050
- I did notice that you
required quieter than normal.

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:15.620
- I felt weird, you know?

00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:18.400
It was just like, because
it's a combination

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:23.400
of getting reintroduced
to something that I feel

00:51:23.500 --> 00:51:28.050
like should be just
like flipping a switch.

00:51:28.050 --> 00:51:29.740
- Well--
- But it wasn't,

00:51:29.740 --> 00:51:31.080
and it wasn't anybody's fault.

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:33.920
And it wasn't, nobody did or said anything

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:35.950
to make me feel weird.

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:39.740
It was just, another part of it

00:51:39.740 --> 00:51:43.640
might just be sheer unmet expectations.

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:46.470
You know, you build something
up and you look forward to it

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:49.460
then you, then you let go of it.

00:51:49.460 --> 00:51:53.150
And then you realize it's
coming back and now there's fear

00:51:53.150 --> 00:51:56.500
in the equation, but
you still have this hope

00:51:56.500 --> 00:52:00.410
that like, this is going to be so dynamic.

00:52:00.410 --> 00:52:05.030
This is going to be, I
mean, I never been to prison

00:52:06.020 --> 00:52:09.620
but I would be interested
to talk to somebody

00:52:09.620 --> 00:52:12.300
who has experienced re-entry from prison.

00:52:12.300 --> 00:52:14.470
- It's probably a little more intense--

00:52:14.470 --> 00:52:19.470
- More intense, but there's
a sliver of parallel,

00:52:19.990 --> 00:52:20.823
at least, you know?

00:52:20.823 --> 00:52:25.820
- I could tell, I made
note that multiple people

00:52:28.250 --> 00:52:30.410
both on Friday night and on Sunday night

00:52:30.410 --> 00:52:32.740
were not acting like themselves.

00:52:32.740 --> 00:52:34.230
- Hm mm.

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:37.280
- And or what I remember of
them in those settings, right.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:38.367
And I did notice that
about you, I was like,

00:52:38.367 --> 00:52:42.230
"Link's being like, typically
he's very talkative.

00:52:42.230 --> 00:52:44.490
Sometimes we have to pull
him back a little bit.

00:52:44.490 --> 00:52:45.397
- Yeah.
- But that is not--

00:52:45.397 --> 00:52:46.580
- And I really thought

00:52:46.580 --> 00:52:47.510
I would have been that happy--
- But that's not happening.

00:52:47.510 --> 00:52:48.540
- Especially with the prep.

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:50.490
I'd be like, I don't want
to come on too strong.

00:52:50.490 --> 00:52:51.913
- And, but, okay.

00:52:52.860 --> 00:52:57.313
But what I ended up
doing was after dinner,

00:52:58.240 --> 00:52:59.680
we're all kind of like hanging out

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:03.450
and I needed to use the restroom.

00:53:03.450 --> 00:53:05.220
So I went upstairs to use it.

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:10.220
And I will do this at most
get-togethers at my house.

00:53:12.310 --> 00:53:15.150
I will go upstairs and then I'll be like,

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:18.090
I'm going to just hang here for a second.

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:20.320
But that was, and I did
that on Sunday night.

00:53:20.320 --> 00:53:22.160
- You did disappear for a little bit.

00:53:22.160 --> 00:53:25.380
- But I always do that
and always did that.

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:27.920
- It wasn't longer than I would believe

00:53:28.810 --> 00:53:30.810
like an involved dump would take.

00:53:30.810 --> 00:53:32.670
- I don't even think it was
a dump, I think I just--

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:33.503
- Oh, I know, I'm just saying--

00:53:33.503 --> 00:53:34.350
- Think I had a pee.

00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:35.880
- The amount of time that you took--

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:36.713
- Was under cover.

00:53:36.713 --> 00:53:38.350
You gotta, you gotta,
you gotta keep in mind

00:53:38.350 --> 00:53:40.040
you don't want to like,
draw attention to how long

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:41.880
you're in the bathroom, but it could,

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:42.810
people start thinking things.

00:53:42.810 --> 00:53:44.590
They start assuming things,

00:53:44.590 --> 00:53:46.570
but that's my introversion.

00:53:46.570 --> 00:53:50.670
That's my, I got, okay,
I got time for people

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:51.760
and I got energy for people

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:52.940
then I have to pull it back.

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:56.170
But actually, as it was
happening, 'cause I'm also

00:53:56.170 --> 00:53:57.490
in my own head a little bit.

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:02.490
I was like, am I having
like, my energy has been

00:54:06.110 --> 00:54:09.080
impacted by this pandemic?

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.650
And I've got low capacity
for people right now?

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:13.630
Then I was like, no, no, no,
you're just an introvert.

00:54:13.630 --> 00:54:15.470
And you did this before the pandemic.

00:54:15.470 --> 00:54:16.303
- Hm mm.

00:54:16.303 --> 00:54:17.240
- So I just kind of went back downstairs.

00:54:17.240 --> 00:54:19.150
But in the midst of the conversation,

00:54:19.150 --> 00:54:23.420
like, all right we're
having a conversation,

00:54:23.420 --> 00:54:26.210
to me, I was also
thinking and noticing it.

00:54:26.210 --> 00:54:29.270
I was like, I kind of
feel like I'm just back

00:54:29.270 --> 00:54:31.990
where we left off but I can tell

00:54:31.990 --> 00:54:34.900
that more than a few people
are not at that place

00:54:34.900 --> 00:54:37.200
because our conversation feels different.

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:38.750
There are more gaps.

00:54:38.750 --> 00:54:40.470
There's more like awkward--

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:43.010
- Why do we keep talking
about dogs so much?

00:54:43.010 --> 00:54:44.240
Like, that's the thing I told Chrissy.

00:54:44.240 --> 00:54:46.793
I was like, you know, I
was disappointed, like,

00:54:46.793 --> 00:54:49.240
I kind of thought we would talk about

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:50.910
what we were experiencing, kind of what

00:54:50.910 --> 00:54:52.110
we're talking about now.

00:54:53.570 --> 00:54:55.020
I feel like I would've had a better time

00:54:55.020 --> 00:54:56.940
if we could have just talked about that.

00:54:56.940 --> 00:54:58.140
But maybe you talked about that

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.833
some on Friday night without us.

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:05.430
But we ended up talking about dogs

00:55:05.430 --> 00:55:08.040
on four different times.

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:10.860
We were over here eating
and then we moved over here

00:55:10.860 --> 00:55:13.830
and then we went inside
and it was just like,

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:15.810
we don't have as much to talk about.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:17.700
We keep going back to dogs.

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:19.680
- I don't know--
- Was, that's what I--

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:23.990
- But, you also, you've
always attached expectations

00:55:23.990 --> 00:55:25.581
to what conversations will be.

00:55:25.581 --> 00:55:26.560
- Yep.

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:28.140
- That was, that's not a pandemic-thing.

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:29.610
That's a Link-thing.

00:55:29.610 --> 00:55:31.140
There's many times, and this is sort

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:33.010
of a gripe between my wife and you.

00:55:33.010 --> 00:55:34.650
This is like one of
the sources of conflict

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:37.080
between Jesse and you is like,

00:55:37.080 --> 00:55:38.590
Jesse will start talking about something,

00:55:38.590 --> 00:55:41.203
and you'll be like, "Ah, uh, can we,

00:55:42.067 --> 00:55:43.520
"let's not talk about that right now?"

00:55:43.520 --> 00:55:44.830
- Right.

00:55:44.830 --> 00:55:47.590
- She normally not dogs.
(Rhett chuckles)

00:55:47.590 --> 00:55:48.890
- Cause like I could have said,

00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:50.557
I think legitimately, "Ah, you you know,

00:55:50.557 --> 00:55:53.645
"we've talked about dogs
three times tonight."

00:55:53.645 --> 00:55:54.861
I'm curious.

00:55:54.861 --> 00:55:56.960
How are you guys doing?

00:55:56.960 --> 00:55:59.600
Cause I feel weird as shit right now.

00:55:59.600 --> 00:56:01.190
You know, it's like--
- Yeah.

00:56:01.190 --> 00:56:03.020
- But I also didn't have the energy

00:56:03.020 --> 00:56:05.310
and I didn't want to
do that at that moment.

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:06.380
But, uh,
(Link chuckles)

00:56:06.380 --> 00:56:10.640
you know, I do own the, the
conversation police thing

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:13.900
because it is an outward manifestation

00:56:13.900 --> 00:56:17.760
of my, my inner critic
and the control thing

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:19.950
that has to do with
expectations associated

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:21.453
with connecting with people.

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:25.263
Whether that, like the dynamics.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:27.617
I think about it too much.

00:56:27.617 --> 00:56:29.540
"Oh, who's included, who's interesting?"

00:56:29.540 --> 00:56:32.068
Can we, you know, are
we... yeah, too much.

00:56:32.068 --> 00:56:33.423
(Rhett chuckles)
Too much for people.

00:56:35.000 --> 00:56:36.740
So on that piece of it, so yeah.

00:56:36.740 --> 00:56:39.290
It's kind of like parsing what,

00:56:39.290 --> 00:56:41.320
what's true of my former self?

00:56:41.320 --> 00:56:43.160
What's new in the mix now,

00:56:43.160 --> 00:56:46.960
what, this being a process, you know,

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:51.960
I even, I did a search because
I know that there's people

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:55.927
who are dealing with this more like,

00:56:55.927 --> 00:57:00.630
and to a greater degree, there's
people who might've already

00:57:00.630 --> 00:57:04.670
been agoraphobic or have
some sort of social anxiety

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:06.877
already that wasn't something
like me, that's like,

00:57:06.877 --> 00:57:08.380
"Did I have that?"
- Yeah.

00:57:08.380 --> 00:57:09.933
- Is that where this is coming from?

00:57:11.110 --> 00:57:14.910
Nearly 50% of Americans say
they feel anxious about resuming

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:18.150
in-person interactions,
post-pandemic, according to a report

00:57:18.150 --> 00:57:21.790
from the American
Psychological Association.

00:57:21.790 --> 00:57:26.790
A phenomenon psychologists
have dubbed "Re-entry Anxiety,"

00:57:27.980 --> 00:57:31.137
and Kiko, put an echo on that, thank you.

00:57:31.137 --> 00:57:34.815
"Re-entry Anxiety."
(echo bouncing)

00:57:34.815 --> 00:57:36.080
(crosstalk)

00:57:36.080 --> 00:57:37.790
- I've heard of a lot of
people talking about it.

00:57:37.790 --> 00:57:39.845
- I just want to go through this

00:57:39.845 --> 00:57:41.000
and I don't wanna get bogged down in it,

00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:46.000
but SCL Health, I think this
is like Colorado Hospital.

00:57:46.660 --> 00:57:47.700
Doesn't matter.

00:57:47.700 --> 00:57:51.720
Very practical advice to deal with

00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:55.390
if you're, if you're struggling
with re-entry anxiety

00:57:56.270 --> 00:57:57.730
just like a handful of things.

00:57:57.730 --> 00:57:59.950
First, they say start small.

00:57:59.950 --> 00:58:02.480
If you haven't left the house
in much over the last year

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:03.990
don't suddenly rip out the band-aid

00:58:03.990 --> 00:58:06.160
and walk into the most crowded
marketplace you can find,

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:09.540
start small, plan a
hike with a few friends

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:14.030
work your way up from
there slowly confronting

00:58:14.030 --> 00:58:18.130
your sources of fear, with a
little more exposure each time.

00:58:18.130 --> 00:58:22.970
Exposure therapy is what
it's called by doctors.

00:58:22.970 --> 00:58:27.970
This next point really
resonated with me, "Start soon."

00:58:29.710 --> 00:58:31.760
If anything is making you anxious,

00:58:31.760 --> 00:58:34.710
you should confront the
issue as soon as possible.

00:58:34.710 --> 00:58:38.810
The longer you wait the
more the anxiety ferments

00:58:38.810 --> 00:58:40.283
and works itself up.

00:58:41.470 --> 00:58:43.410
So not only does social isolation

00:58:43.410 --> 00:58:45.480
have short term health
implications that can impact

00:58:45.480 --> 00:58:48.040
mental health long-term, it doesn't mean

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:49.240
you should throw a party

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:53.010
and you need to be subject to

00:58:53.010 --> 00:58:54.490
the guidelines as we said up front.

00:58:54.490 --> 00:58:59.490
But a little socialization
goes a long way.

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:05.297
You got to say, "I'm
engaging in this process

00:59:05.297 --> 00:59:10.297
"of re-entry and I have to take baby steps

00:59:10.297 --> 00:59:14.080
"and I need to start soon, or now.

00:59:14.080 --> 00:59:17.347
Another another thing
was, piece of advice was

00:59:17.347 --> 00:59:19.177
"Looking at the big picture."

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:24.397
A good thing to do is to ask yourself,

00:59:24.397 --> 00:59:27.370
"What truly makes me happy."

00:59:27.370 --> 00:59:30.720
And whether that's family, the outdoors,

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:32.630
returning back to life,

00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:34.970
let the big picture be a major motivator

00:59:34.970 --> 00:59:39.920
to overcome the fears
in comparison to them,

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:43.600
in combating reasons
to not start or delay.

00:59:43.600 --> 00:59:46.090
You know, I was gearing up for Sunday.

00:59:46.090 --> 00:59:47.910
I wasn't ready for Friday.

00:59:47.910 --> 00:59:50.370
I listened to myself and you know

00:59:51.490 --> 00:59:56.490
but if there wasn't a Sunday plan, I'd,

00:59:56.590 --> 00:59:59.340
I might would've felt differently now

00:59:59.340 --> 01:00:01.847
about my decision to not go on Friday.

01:00:01.847 --> 01:00:02.907
- Hm mm.

01:00:02.907 --> 01:00:05.570
- And then two more things, buddy up,

01:00:05.570 --> 01:00:10.570
find someone you know and
trust who's in a similar phase

01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:12.960
or has a similar experience
and understands you

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:15.130
that you can buddy up with
and support each other

01:00:15.130 --> 01:00:18.650
through the re-entry
process, talking about it.

01:00:18.650 --> 01:00:20.713
I mean, even this
conversation is freeing for me

01:00:20.713 --> 01:00:23.127
because it's like, "Hey, you've heard

01:00:23.127 --> 01:00:24.160
"what's inside of my head."

01:00:24.160 --> 01:00:25.720
I've kind of given you this emotional

01:00:25.720 --> 01:00:27.780
brain-dump so to speak.

01:00:27.780 --> 01:00:32.780
So it's, I think the next time we're like,

01:00:33.690 --> 01:00:37.393
if it's next weekend, that
we're in a small group setting,

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:42.000
there might be an opportunity
for you to say something

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:43.980
to me or me to say
something to somebody else

01:00:43.980 --> 01:00:45.650
who's opened up to me, that type of thing,

01:00:45.650 --> 01:00:47.510
you know, I think it really helps.

01:00:47.510 --> 01:00:50.940
And then yeah, they, they note in the end

01:00:50.940 --> 01:00:53.420
for some people it's going to be a breeze

01:00:53.420 --> 01:00:56.693
like the way you described
it could potentially,

01:00:58.600 --> 01:01:03.600
I could experience jealousy
or envy or, or beat myself up

01:01:05.060 --> 01:01:08.210
because it's not as easy
for me as it is for you.

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:10.790
And that's no fault of yours.

01:01:10.790 --> 01:01:15.080
And this is my responsibility to process

01:01:15.080 --> 01:01:18.470
that in a way that doesn't, you know

01:01:18.470 --> 01:01:22.790
drive a wedge between us and
somehow then send a message

01:01:22.790 --> 01:01:26.920
that you can't enjoy
your re-entry experience.

01:01:26.920 --> 01:01:27.753
That's not--

01:01:27.753 --> 01:01:29.287
- Well it remains to be seen--
- That's not fair to you.

01:01:29.287 --> 01:01:30.900
- Well it remains to be seen as well as--

01:01:30.900 --> 01:01:31.733
- Oh, but hold on.

01:01:31.733 --> 01:01:32.680
I just wanted to make one...

01:01:32.680 --> 01:01:34.470
But the main point I was trying to make

01:01:34.470 --> 01:01:36.390
that they were making at the end was

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:40.220
if you come to a roadblock,
you can seek out help.

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:42.840
That's beyond just a friend, you know,

01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:44.610
a professional--
- Yeah!

01:01:44.610 --> 01:01:45.900
- Help to get you through this.

01:01:45.900 --> 01:01:47.720
And ain't no shame in that.

01:01:47.720 --> 01:01:48.553
What were you gonna say?

01:01:48.553 --> 01:01:50.263
- I think the thing that I am,

01:01:51.340 --> 01:01:53.570
I'm not going to be
surprised if I experienced

01:01:53.570 --> 01:01:58.570
some anxiety, I'm interested
to see, is a big crowd.

01:02:03.520 --> 01:02:07.180
Like if we're going to a basketball game

01:02:07.180 --> 01:02:11.010
and there's a herd of people
going through the small door

01:02:11.010 --> 01:02:12.170
and then they're all crowded together.

01:02:12.170 --> 01:02:17.070
Like I can only assume that
when that kind of thing

01:02:17.070 --> 01:02:19.810
starts happening more and more often,

01:02:19.810 --> 01:02:22.530
so not socially distance, large gatherings

01:02:22.530 --> 01:02:25.040
people packed into places,
people at concerts.

01:02:25.040 --> 01:02:25.950
- Yeah.

01:02:25.950 --> 01:02:27.060
- People are going to panic.

01:02:27.060 --> 01:02:30.540
You know, I think the
reasons for the anxiety,

01:02:30.540 --> 01:02:34.420
they may not be rational,
but a lot of them

01:02:34.420 --> 01:02:35.810
they probably come from different places.

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:37.770
Like I can imagine that--
- Sure.

01:02:37.770 --> 01:02:40.110
- One of them is just
like, I'm seeing a lot

01:02:40.110 --> 01:02:41.980
of people close to me, touching things.

01:02:41.980 --> 01:02:44.509
I'm watching their mouth move...

01:02:44.509 --> 01:02:45.500
- Oh gosh, the mouths!

01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:47.250
Just the scene of mouths--

01:02:47.250 --> 01:02:48.310
- People touching things.

01:02:48.310 --> 01:02:50.500
And so if that's something
that you struggle with

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:55.330
is just the idea of the,
the germs of it all,

01:02:55.330 --> 01:02:57.110
the humanity of it all.

01:02:57.110 --> 01:02:57.943
- Yeah.

01:02:57.943 --> 01:02:58.900
- Then that could be something.

01:02:58.900 --> 01:03:01.130
And then it just may be,
oh, a social anxiety.

01:03:01.130 --> 01:03:03.160
I don't know how to say the words

01:03:03.160 --> 01:03:05.870
that I used to say when I meet people,

01:03:05.870 --> 01:03:09.430
but that whole surrounded by people,

01:03:09.430 --> 01:03:11.810
I'm not saying I anticipate
it's going to be a problem.

01:03:11.810 --> 01:03:15.830
But if, to me, I feel
like if I'm going to have

01:03:17.090 --> 01:03:20.380
an anxiety issue, I doubt
that it's going to be with

01:03:20.380 --> 01:03:23.480
my friends that I see on
a pretty regular basis

01:03:23.480 --> 01:03:25.210
but it might be in a group of strangers.

01:03:25.210 --> 01:03:26.217
Like that could be where something like,

01:03:26.217 --> 01:03:30.690
"Oh, all of a sudden this,
this is such a new experience."

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:33.220
And again, I mean, I guess there...

01:03:33.220 --> 01:03:34.610
It depends on where
you're at in the world.

01:03:34.610 --> 01:03:37.350
It depends on where
you're at in the country.

01:03:37.350 --> 01:03:39.960
These are, I'm sure there
are people listening

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:42.057
to this podcast who are in
States where they're like

01:03:42.057 --> 01:03:44.460
"Ah, it's kinda just been the
same for a long time for us,"

01:03:44.460 --> 01:03:47.830
guys are like, "You guys are
crazy and California is nuts."

01:03:47.830 --> 01:03:50.335
- We're proud and we're
affirming their decisions--

01:03:50.335 --> 01:03:52.701
- Yeah!
- All along the way.

01:03:52.701 --> 01:03:54.657
And it's like, "Well,
we're not trying to affirm

01:03:54.657 --> 01:03:57.317
"your decision if you didn't,
if you didn't lock down

01:03:57.317 --> 01:03:58.710
"or wear a mask or whatever."

01:03:58.710 --> 01:03:59.870
- But uh...
- Yeah.

01:03:59.870 --> 01:04:01.480
- So yeah, when it comes to parties,

01:04:01.480 --> 01:04:04.760
like with friends I'm
committed to like getting back

01:04:04.760 --> 01:04:08.630
to that magic spot but
then like then beyond that,

01:04:08.630 --> 01:04:11.517
like, okay, 'cause to
me it's, it's not about

01:04:11.517 --> 01:04:15.570
catching anything or exposure or the like,

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:16.900
I dunno, I don't worry about that.

01:04:16.900 --> 01:04:18.250
It is the social thing.

01:04:18.250 --> 01:04:21.060
And like, it's just like
getting reacquainted with that.

01:04:21.060 --> 01:04:24.050
So it was like the parties
with strangers, sorry.

01:04:24.050 --> 01:04:24.883
Oh yeah.

01:04:24.883 --> 01:04:27.940
It's like I was on the fence about

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:29.560
how much I enjoyed that beforehand, so--

01:04:29.560 --> 01:04:31.509
- Well, that's the thing
that's kind of odd to me

01:04:31.509 --> 01:04:34.050
is we, you know, we've done

01:04:34.050 --> 01:04:36.400
we've done at least an episode of the show

01:04:36.400 --> 01:04:39.400
about our approach to Hollywood parties.

01:04:39.400 --> 01:04:40.860
And I'm not talking about
the Fauci-parties where

01:04:40.860 --> 01:04:42.270
I drink the children's blood.

01:04:42.270 --> 01:04:44.090
I'm talking about like industry parties,

01:04:44.090 --> 01:04:45.810
where you dress up and you have cocktails

01:04:45.810 --> 01:04:46.869
and their music is too loud.

01:04:46.869 --> 01:04:49.420
Both neither of us have ever been a fan

01:04:49.420 --> 01:04:51.590
of those kinds of parties
because of the whole,

01:04:51.590 --> 01:04:54.470
I can't have a conversation
with you right now.

01:04:54.470 --> 01:04:56.650
Cause I can't hear you
because of the music

01:04:56.650 --> 01:05:01.020
and I, is this for clout
or what is the reason

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:02.270
I'm walking around this party?

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:03.200
Who am I trying to talk to?

01:05:03.200 --> 01:05:05.240
Am I trying to tell John
Mayer that I love him?

01:05:05.240 --> 01:05:08.990
Or I might just trying to meet somebody

01:05:08.990 --> 01:05:10.125
that I might work with...

01:05:10.125 --> 01:05:12.120
And--
- Both.

01:05:12.120 --> 01:05:17.120
- As we've established,
you tend to be comfortable

01:05:17.840 --> 01:05:19.130
with being like, well,
I'm just going to go

01:05:19.130 --> 01:05:21.514
up to a little Dicky and
start a conversation.

01:05:21.514 --> 01:05:23.860
And I'm like, well, I'll let
you go up the little Dickie

01:05:23.860 --> 01:05:24.790
and start a conversation.

01:05:24.790 --> 01:05:27.609
And then once you're in,
I'll come and join it, right?

01:05:27.609 --> 01:05:30.740
As we hilariously told the story one time.

01:05:30.740 --> 01:05:34.180
But interestingly, it's so
funny that the whole story

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:35.880
about little Dicky
involves him telling you

01:05:35.880 --> 01:05:36.870
that his name is Dave.

01:05:36.870 --> 01:05:39.264
And now the show that he
makes, is just called Dave.

01:05:39.264 --> 01:05:41.060
(Rhet laughing)
- Yeah.

01:05:41.060 --> 01:05:42.870
- The way that it play, every time I see

01:05:42.870 --> 01:05:45.516
that show advertised, I just
think about that story, but--

01:05:45.516 --> 01:05:48.250
- I can't bring myself to
watch it for that reason.

01:05:48.250 --> 01:05:51.950
- I, my prediction is that
everyone's going to have

01:05:51.950 --> 01:05:55.770
some latent sort of anxiety in this.

01:05:55.770 --> 01:05:58.123
And again, I, I'm not I'm not minimizing,

01:05:59.097 --> 01:05:59.930
there's people who are really struggling

01:05:59.930 --> 01:06:01.810
and this is a serious thing

01:06:01.810 --> 01:06:04.350
and they are going to need
to get professional help.

01:06:04.350 --> 01:06:05.950
I understand that, that's not my scenario.

01:06:05.950 --> 01:06:07.000
That's not my situation.

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:09.530
And I have empathy for the
people who are going through it.

01:06:09.530 --> 01:06:12.226
But I think that
everybody is going to have

01:06:12.226 --> 01:06:14.760
there's going to be
awkwardness when you go back

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:17.770
to that party with the music
and the talking to each other

01:06:17.770 --> 01:06:20.660
which was already difficult
for everyone involved,

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:23.693
even though we tend to not
talk about how difficult it is.

01:06:23.693 --> 01:06:25.707
Throw in the fact that like,

01:06:25.707 --> 01:06:28.427
"I haven't been in an
environment this loud

01:06:28.427 --> 01:06:30.810
"let alone with this many people"!

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:32.010
When that ha--

01:06:32.010 --> 01:06:37.010
My hope is that out of a, in
an effort to kind of help us

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:40.767
all ease back in, that
the DJ will be like,

01:06:40.767 --> 01:06:42.870
"Man, I'm going to go a
little easy on these people."

01:06:42.870 --> 01:06:43.936
- I'm telling you--
- You know what I'm saying?

01:06:43.936 --> 01:06:44.769
Cause I just think that
there's so much opportunity

01:06:44.769 --> 01:06:47.710
for parties to be transformed.

01:06:47.710 --> 01:06:48.857
I mean, we did a whole chapter of this

01:06:48.857 --> 01:06:50.240
in "The Book of Mythicality," about how

01:06:50.240 --> 01:06:52.090
we didn't like loud parties.

01:06:52.090 --> 01:06:54.910
I just feel like there's
an opportunity here

01:06:54.910 --> 01:06:58.780
to not just go back to the bullshit party

01:06:58.780 --> 01:07:00.420
where you can't hear anybody.

01:07:00.420 --> 01:07:02.590
- Yeah, I got more thoughts
on this that relate

01:07:02.590 --> 01:07:06.490
to something else that
we've been talking about

01:07:06.490 --> 01:07:08.470
and laying groundwork for...

01:07:08.470 --> 01:07:09.938
- But you're not going
to bring it up now--

01:07:09.938 --> 01:07:10.771
- No.

01:07:10.771 --> 01:07:11.604
- You're thinking of
bringing it up later--

01:07:11.604 --> 01:07:12.437
- I'm just telling you so that

01:07:12.437 --> 01:07:13.270
we won't forget to talk about it--

01:07:13.270 --> 01:07:14.353
- Oh!
- Later.

01:07:15.680 --> 01:07:18.550
Another, you know, I talked
about the prison and prisoner

01:07:18.550 --> 01:07:22.810
being released-analogy, but
it's also thinking about

01:07:22.810 --> 01:07:25.720
like an astronaut who's been
in the International Space

01:07:25.720 --> 01:07:28.850
Station for like a year and
then they come back to Earth

01:07:28.850 --> 01:07:31.420
and they're like, even
though they've worked out

01:07:31.420 --> 01:07:34.250
and everything and they've stayed healthy

01:07:34.250 --> 01:07:37.580
and they've been focused on
what it is they've got to do,

01:07:37.580 --> 01:07:39.520
then they come back to this world

01:07:39.520 --> 01:07:44.270
and all the gravity
takes its toll you know?

01:07:44.270 --> 01:07:46.840
And it, it takes a while
to like even figure out how

01:07:46.840 --> 01:07:50.320
to physically walk properly.

01:07:50.320 --> 01:07:51.220
I think?

01:07:51.220 --> 01:07:52.233
Doesn't that happen.
- I'm sure, yeah.

01:07:52.233 --> 01:07:54.600
Depends on how long you've been up there.

01:07:54.600 --> 01:07:55.877
- And relate to people, you know?

01:07:55.877 --> 01:07:57.360
And so I'd be interested to see

01:07:57.360 --> 01:07:59.740
what they're saying about that.

01:07:59.740 --> 01:08:02.520
I'd take a, there is an
astronaut masterclass

01:08:02.520 --> 01:08:04.603
but I don't think it's related to this.

01:08:04.603 --> 01:08:05.436
- A masterclass?

01:08:05.436 --> 01:08:08.980
- Yeah, so I'm interested in one of those.

01:08:08.980 --> 01:08:11.730
Let's bring on an astronaut to talk about

01:08:11.730 --> 01:08:13.990
how to re-enter the world socially.

01:08:13.990 --> 01:08:14.823
- Not sure that...

01:08:14.823 --> 01:08:15.656
Oh, well, okay.

01:08:15.656 --> 01:08:17.570
If he, if he makes the
connection to that, yeah.

01:08:17.570 --> 01:08:20.580
- Okay. Or she, women can be Astronauts.

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:21.413
- That's true.

01:08:21.413 --> 01:08:23.260
If they make the connection to that.

01:08:23.260 --> 01:08:25.650
- There we go!
(Link chuckles)

01:08:25.650 --> 01:08:26.483
- So,

01:08:30.640 --> 01:08:31.670
this is just kind of

01:08:31.670 --> 01:08:33.440
put in a capper

01:08:33.440 --> 01:08:37.260
at least this is how I hope
we're rounding a corner

01:08:37.260 --> 01:08:42.260
that like, you know there's
not going to be any,

01:08:42.550 --> 01:08:46.310
we don't have to return
to any type of lockdown.

01:08:46.310 --> 01:08:48.020
I don't even want to talk about that.

01:08:48.020 --> 01:08:51.440
I'm hoping this is a bookend

01:08:51.440 --> 01:08:53.970
on an experience that
if you, if you go back

01:08:53.970 --> 01:08:55.480
and listen to our conversations,

01:08:55.480 --> 01:08:57.020
as we were entering into it

01:08:57.020 --> 01:08:59.307
we were thinking of things like,

01:08:59.307 --> 01:09:02.400
"Okay, we're living history."

01:09:02.400 --> 01:09:05.093
There are opportunities
associated with this.

01:09:06.710 --> 01:09:08.690
Let's embrace this moment for what it is.

01:09:08.690 --> 01:09:13.240
Let's be as responsible
and healthy as possible

01:09:14.120 --> 01:09:18.010
but let's also embrace
the experience of it.

01:09:18.010 --> 01:09:22.680
So when we're done with
it we have lessons from it

01:09:22.680 --> 01:09:24.640
and we can move forward.

01:09:24.640 --> 01:09:25.980
I'm not ready to say that yet

01:09:25.980 --> 01:09:28.920
because we're realizing
that we're still having...

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:31.930
The re-entry is an
experience in and of itself

01:09:31.930 --> 01:09:36.260
that I want to have that mentality

01:09:36.260 --> 01:09:38.550
of "I'm embracing this moment by moment

01:09:39.687 --> 01:09:44.687
"as part of history
and giving myself grace

01:09:46.390 --> 01:09:50.970
to be on that process of reentry."

01:09:50.970 --> 01:09:54.790
That it it's okay that
it's not flipping a switch

01:09:54.790 --> 01:09:56.120
for every single person.

01:09:56.120 --> 01:10:00.720
And that we're not on the same pace

01:10:00.720 --> 01:10:04.290
but we are on pretty
much the same trajectory

01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:08.450
of moving past this and
the ones that we love,

01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:10.430
some of them need an extra hand

01:10:10.430 --> 01:10:12.550
or a little more understanding,

01:10:12.550 --> 01:10:13.990
more patience to get there.

01:10:13.990 --> 01:10:17.290
- Well I think the fact that a lot of us,

01:10:17.290 --> 01:10:20.110
a lot more people can relate,

01:10:20.110 --> 01:10:25.000
people like you, as an example
that wouldn't have said

01:10:25.000 --> 01:10:27.675
that you had any sort of
social anxiety who are like,

01:10:27.675 --> 01:10:29.500
"Is this what I'm experiencing now?"

01:10:29.500 --> 01:10:33.440
I think a large number of
people experiencing that,

01:10:33.440 --> 01:10:36.410
is a great opportunity
for more people to relate

01:10:36.410 --> 01:10:39.680
to those who had social anxiety disorders

01:10:39.680 --> 01:10:41.110
before the pandemic started.

01:10:41.110 --> 01:10:43.780
- And to astronauts.
- And to astronauts.

01:10:43.780 --> 01:10:46.470
- So we can, you know,
never hurts to have a little

01:10:46.470 --> 01:10:50.040
more empathy in the world and to have

01:10:50.040 --> 01:10:54.253
those conversations about
other people's perspectives.

01:10:55.110 --> 01:10:57.448
- Yeah, it's my rec.

01:10:57.448 --> 01:10:58.860
Imma give it to you.

01:10:58.860 --> 01:11:03.860
I recommend this week that you
go through your old shoes--

01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:07.990
- Hmm.

01:11:07.990 --> 01:11:10.330
- Put on a pair of shoes you haven't worn

01:11:10.330 --> 01:11:11.860
since the beginning of pandemic, you know

01:11:11.860 --> 01:11:13.200
you got those shoes that are in the,

01:11:13.200 --> 01:11:14.810
you shoved them to the back of somewhere

01:11:14.810 --> 01:11:16.740
and you're like, "I'll bring
these out for something"

01:11:16.740 --> 01:11:18.450
I'm not necessarily
talking about dress shoes.

01:11:18.450 --> 01:11:19.490
It doesn't matter.

01:11:19.490 --> 01:11:20.323
Whatever.

01:11:20.323 --> 01:11:22.630
Walk a mile in someone else's shoes.

01:11:22.630 --> 01:11:24.900
That's someone else
being your former self.

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:28.430
I think it's, I don't know.

01:11:28.430 --> 01:11:33.430
It's just like a practical,
physical, active embodiment

01:11:34.080 --> 01:11:37.290
of reconnecting with your former self.

01:11:37.290 --> 01:11:40.970
And then after walking
in those shoes all day

01:11:40.970 --> 01:11:42.930
you realize why you haven't
worn them in a year.

01:11:42.930 --> 01:11:45.060
And it has nothing to
do with the pandemic.

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:47.510
Then either give them to Goodwill

01:11:47.510 --> 01:11:50.193
or if they're really horrible,
just throw them away.

01:11:52.160 --> 01:11:54.410
And let it go.
- Hmm.

01:11:54.410 --> 01:11:57.830
- And consider it an act of embodiment,

01:11:57.830 --> 01:12:00.870
an exercise in remembering
your former self

01:12:00.870 --> 01:12:03.240
but also deciding that
you're not obligated

01:12:03.240 --> 01:12:06.820
to still be that person moving forward.

01:12:06.820 --> 01:12:08.800
Maybe there's things you wanna change.

01:12:08.800 --> 01:12:09.823
You're looking at my shoes.

01:12:09.823 --> 01:12:10.930
- I was thinking you were
like wearing like a different,

01:12:10.930 --> 01:12:12.840
I haven't seen you wear a
different pair of shoes.

01:12:12.840 --> 01:12:14.480
- I don't take the advice I give.

01:12:14.480 --> 01:12:16.238
Oh, okay.
(Rhett chuckles)

01:12:16.238 --> 01:12:17.920
(upbeat music)

01:12:17.920 --> 01:12:19.443
- Walk a mile in someone else's shoes.

01:12:19.443 --> 01:12:20.450
- I think is good advice.

01:12:20.450 --> 01:12:22.020
I think there's a third option--

01:12:22.020 --> 01:12:24.211
- Do it!
- That somebody just

01:12:24.211 --> 01:12:26.220
told me about 'cause I,
cause I have a tendency

01:12:26.220 --> 01:12:28.250
to go through all my
old clothes and shoes.

01:12:28.250 --> 01:12:29.083
- Yeah?

01:12:29.083 --> 01:12:30.860
- And then donate all of them

01:12:30.860 --> 01:12:32.040
which I think that that is,

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:34.020
priority number one is to donate.

01:12:34.020 --> 01:12:35.680
But somebody said, you know

01:12:35.680 --> 01:12:38.930
you should save some
stuff that's more notable

01:12:38.930 --> 01:12:40.980
and indicative of the time period

01:12:40.980 --> 01:12:43.720
because your kids will want
to wear it at some point

01:12:43.720 --> 01:12:45.820
or want to be like this
is my dad's old shirt

01:12:45.820 --> 01:12:47.210
from 20 years ago.

01:12:47.210 --> 01:12:50.510
These are my dad's
shoes from 20 years ago.

01:12:50.510 --> 01:12:52.360
And I think that there's that again,

01:12:52.360 --> 01:12:54.810
I give away most of it

01:12:54.810 --> 01:12:59.410
but keep a few pieces as
memorabilia for your children.

01:12:59.410 --> 01:13:00.990
Somebody just gave me that advice.

01:13:00.990 --> 01:13:01.990
And I don't remember who it was,

01:13:01.990 --> 01:13:03.150
but I was like, that's a good idea

01:13:03.150 --> 01:13:05.390
because I don't have, like I think about

01:13:05.390 --> 01:13:06.870
the stuff that I wore in college

01:13:06.870 --> 01:13:08.830
and I see it in these pictures, but like,

01:13:08.830 --> 01:13:10.430
it'd be cool if I could be like,

01:13:11.557 --> 01:13:13.767
"Look, here's the shirt
I wore in college."

01:13:13.767 --> 01:13:14.934
"You're wanna wear in college?"

01:13:14.934 --> 01:13:15.767
(Rhett chuckles)

01:13:15.767 --> 01:13:19.500
Or just do you want it
just as a piece of history?

01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:20.604
- So keep 'em,

01:13:20.604 --> 01:13:21.437
(Rhett laughs)

01:13:21.437 --> 01:13:22.270
for that reason.

01:13:22.270 --> 01:13:23.660
- No, give away most of them

01:13:23.660 --> 01:13:24.760
- Keep some of them...

01:13:25.650 --> 01:13:27.950
- The experiences is
the more important part?

01:13:27.950 --> 01:13:29.045
- You're right.

01:13:29.045 --> 01:13:30.710
- It's not really about what
you do with them afterwards.

01:13:30.710 --> 01:13:31.660
- It just hit me, you know?

01:13:31.660 --> 01:13:33.227
- Okay, all right.

01:13:33.227 --> 01:13:35.660
- #EarBiscuits, let us
know what you think.

01:13:35.660 --> 01:13:38.000
What's your experience on re-entry?

01:13:38.000 --> 01:13:40.863
- And I am, genuinely interested

01:13:40.863 --> 01:13:45.863
if there's anybody who, what
I had to say about vaccines

01:13:47.384 --> 01:13:50.040
made you think something
new and different.

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:52.660
I would, I continue to be interested

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:56.480
in being able to be a force for good.

01:13:56.480 --> 01:14:00.280
And I want to know if I just
pissed a bunch of people off,

01:14:00.280 --> 01:14:01.967
didn't change anybody's
mind, or if you're like,

01:14:01.967 --> 01:14:04.120
"Oh, actually I'm going to do this now."

01:14:04.120 --> 01:14:08.040
I just, from my own personal data points

01:14:08.040 --> 01:14:11.210
let me know or just tell
me why, why I'm wrong.

01:14:11.210 --> 01:14:12.787
I'm also very open to that.

01:14:12.787 --> 01:14:13.943
#EarBiscuits.

